[Music]  welcome back so in the last lecture I  introduced the discrete Fourier  transform which is a matrix transform  that takes you from a vector of data  sampled at regular intervals in space or  time  to a vector of Fourier coefficients of  complex Fourier coefficients for that  data okay so this is the discrete  analogue of the Fourier series which  would approximate this this function  using sines and cosines of higher and  higher frequency the discrete Fourier  transform approximates this data using  sines and cosines with discrete  frequencies that are increasing from  from from 0 to n minus 1 okay so we  derived this discrete Fourier transform  matrix in terms of this fundamental  frequency Omega n where Omega n was e to  the minus 2 pi I over N ok that's kind  of the the fundamental frequency that  you can get on this interval with n data  points ok and now what I'm going to show  you is how to compute this DFT matrix in  MATLAB and in Python so just huge  caution you almost never actually want  to compute the discrete Fourier  transform matrix you always compute the  discrete Fourier transform using the  fast Fourier transform algorithm pretty  much always I'm just showing you this  because it looks cool and because I  wanted to show you how you would  actually build this if for some reason  you wanted to okay now some things to  point out this discrete Fourier  transform matrix has some really  important properties this is what's  known as a vandermonde matrix it is also  a unitary matrix so just like in the  singular value decomposition we saw that  unitary matrices were really important  this is kind of the original really  important unitary matrix is the discrete  Fourier transform and unitary again  means that if you have two high  dimensional vectors that have given  inner product in in real space when I  map them through into this discrete  Fourier transform coordinate system I  still have the same  product okay so it preserves angles and  distances through this map very  important property that's what allows us  to approximate data using these Fourier  transforms efficiently okay so we're  gonna see a lot more about this later  but here I'm just going to show you how  to code this up in MATLAB and Python  it's really simple in pink here you can  see this is kind of the slow kind of  easy way to compute it would be with two  for loops you just go through I and  through J to compute these these  coefficients just like I've done here in  terms of this fundamental frequency W or  Omega this is a better way of computing  this more efficiently or faster where  you basically create a grid an N by n  array of IJ locations and then you  compute Omega to all of those indexes  all at once using this dot ^ I minus 1  times J minus 1 okay so what this is is  I and J are both big arrays of every I  and J index over this whole array and  you can simultaneously take W to all of  those powers at once in this vectorized  operation in MATLAB so vectorized  operations are always much much faster  so this is kind of the easy human  readable code this is how you would  actually compute it fast in MATLAB and  now I'm gonna show you just the real  part of this DFT matrix so remember this  is a complex valued matrix because these  Fourier coefficients have a magnitude  and a phase they don't just tell you how  much of that sine or cosine it tells you  what's the phase so of course you know I  have maybe my fundamental frequency the  magnitude tells you how big to scale it  but the phase tells you if it should be  shifted to one side or to the other so  the phase and the magnitude are both  important and they're both captured by  this complex Fourier coefficient and so  I'm just gonna plot you the real part of  this this DFT matrix okay good that's  right here and so I'm gonna make this  much bigger  so this is what my discrete for you  transform matrix looks like okay you can  see kind of there's a lot of interesting  patterns here there's bands of like you  know positive negative positive negative  or like big small a lot of really  interesting structure in this 1024 by  1024 matrix one of the things I think is  kind of fun is that if you change the  size of this when you're looking at it  on a computer screen when you're looking  at this on a computer screen then then  you'll get some interesting aliasing  effects so here I'm just gonna slowly  vary the size of this thing and you're  gonna see notice that the cells it  almost looks like there are cells  forming and as I change the size of this  thing you can see kind of this  interesting aliasing pattern let me see  if I can get a really good one yeah okay  it's about to happen yes you can see all  these really interesting patterns now it  looks like there's three cells in the  vertical direction here I can probably  make it more or less anyway this is  essentially because there's only so many  pixels on the screen and this is exactly  1024 by 1024 matrix and so yeah if you  make it smaller it's actually really  cool so essentially if I try to  represent this 1024 by 1024 matrix on a  smaller number of pixels then there's  some aliasing and this just points to  the fact that there is so much symmetry  and structure in this Fourier transform  matrix because you know each row is very  similar to the previous row it's kind of  off by a factor of Omega and there's all  of this this structure in this matrix  and you can kind of see lots of  interesting structure here okay now this  is really easy to compute in Python as  well and it's pretty much exactly the  same you create a mesh grid you can  pre-compute a power and here again we're  plotting the real part of this discrete  Fourier transform matrix okay  now I'll just point out that in real  life you're almost never going to  actually compute this DFT matrix that's  not how you're actually gonna Fourier  transform data you're going to use the  fast Fourier transform algorithm  I just thought 